\newlabel{solowkgr}{{2.1}{6}{$k^*$的黄金律值\footnote {The 'Golden Rule' level of capital, $k^*_{GR}$, refers to the steady-state capital stock per effective worker that maximizes consumption per effective worker. While higher capital always means higher output, it also requires higher investment (savings) to maintain. The Golden Rule strikes the optimal balance. If $k < k^*_{GR}$, increasing savings (and thus $k$) would increase future consumption. If $k > k^*_{GR}$ (dynamic inefficiency), reducing savings would decrease $k$ but increase consumption both now and in the future, meaning the economy has over-accumulated capital from a consumption perspective.}}{example.2.1}{}}
